\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The warp dipole is held by a localized exotic core, with negligible ordinary matter.} Left: conformal factor $\chi $ at $z=0$, $t=16$ (bright $=$ expanded, dark $=$ compressed). Right: the geometric required energy density $\rho _{\rm  req}$ of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:rho_req} on the same slice (red $<0$ exotic, blue $>0$ ordinary, shared symmetric scale). The energy-condition violation needed to sustain the geometry is concentrated in one compact core ($\min \rho _{\rm  req}\approx -1.0\times 10^{-3}$) coincident with the compression crescent; the surrounding domain is near-vacuum and the faint outer ring is the weak outgoing $K$-wave (junk radiation), not localized matter.}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rho_req}{{2}{6}{\textbf {The warp dipole is held by a localized exotic core, with negligible ordinary matter.} Left: conformal factor $\chi $ at $z=0$, $t=16$ (bright $=$ expanded, dark $=$ compressed). Right: the geometric required energy density $\rho _{\rm req}$ of Eq.~\eqref {eq:rho_req} on the same slice (red $<0$ exotic, blue $>0$ ordinary, shared symmetric scale). The energy-condition violation needed to sustain the geometry is concentrated in one compact core ($\min \rho _{\rm req}\approx -1.0\times 10^{-3}$) coincident with the compression crescent; the surrounding domain is near-vacuum and the faint outer ring is the weak outgoing $K$-wave (junk radiation), not localized matter}{figure.2}{}}
