\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {III}{\ignorespaces Candidates ranked by the gated objective $S$ (Eq.~\ref {eq:gated_score}). The three top gauge-angular search winners (Tier-1, $t=2$, $N_{\rm  full}=64$) and the high-quality long-run validation of the best ($t=16$, $N_{\rm  full}=192$) are compared to the reference seeds ($t=16$, $N_{\rm  full}=64$). $\DOTSI \intop \ilimits@ \rho _-\,dV$ is the integrated required negative-energy density (geometric requirement from the vacuum Hamiltonian, Eq.~\ref {eq:rho_req}; for the dipole winner it is a single localized core, Fig.~\ref {fig:rho_req}); $\max c$ is the peak coordinate light speed on the initial slice; $F_{\rm  op}^{\rm  ev}$ is the evolved (persistence) operational-FTL benefit. The Tier-1 search $S$ is evaluated over a short window and is therefore optimistic relative to the long-time value---the validated $t=16$ row ($S=16.75$) is the fair comparison with the seeds, and it still outscores Alcubierre. The non-spherical winners use \emph  {normal} matter ($\rho \ge 0$ constrained solve) with low residual exotic content; the reference wormhole/puncture seeds use phantom (exotic) initial data, and Alcubierre's matter sector is clean ($\DOTSI \intop \ilimits@ \rho _-\approx 0$) with its exoticity living in the geometry (effective EC).}}{7}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:score_comparison}{{III}{7}{Candidates ranked by the gated objective $S$ (Eq.~\ref {eq:gated_score}). The three top gauge-angular search winners (Tier-1, $t=2$, $N_{\rm full}=64$) and the high-quality long-run validation of the best ($t=16$, $N_{\rm full}=192$) are compared to the reference seeds ($t=16$, $N_{\rm full}=64$). $\int \rho _-\,dV$ is the integrated required negative-energy density (geometric requirement from the vacuum Hamiltonian, Eq.~\ref {eq:rho_req}; for the dipole winner it is a single localized core, Fig.~\ref {fig:rho_req}); $\max c$ is the peak coordinate light speed on the initial slice; $F_{\rm op}^{\rm ev}$ is the evolved (persistence) operational-FTL benefit. The Tier-1 search $S$ is evaluated over a short window and is therefore optimistic relative to the long-time value---the validated $t=16$ row ($S=16.75$) is the fair comparison with the seeds, and it still outscores Alcubierre. The non-spherical winners use \emph {normal} matter ($\rho \ge 0$ constrained solve) with low residual exotic content; the reference wormhole/puncture seeds use phantom (exotic) initial data, and Alcubierre's matter sector is clean ($\int \rho _-\approx 0$) with its exoticity living in the geometry (effective EC)}{table.3}{}}
